      "text_raw": "The gain at low frequencies\nis easily calculated. Indeed,\nthe circular current, gener-\nated by the input devices, is\namplified by B and flows in\nthe output load.\nThe output resistance R\nn4\nat node 4 is quite high.\nActually it is the only high\nresistance in the circuit. All\nother nodes are at the 1/g\nm\nlevel. As a result, this is a\nsingle-stage amplifier: there\nis only one high-resistance\nnode, one single node where\nthe gain is large, where the\nswing is large, and ultimately where the dominant pole is formed.\nThe voltage gain at low frequencies is now easily obtained.\nThe bandwidth is created at the same output node. The GBW is the product. It is the same\nas for a single-transistor amplifier but multiplied by current factor B.\nIncreasing B increases the GBW.\nHow far can we go with B?",
      "text": "The gain at low frequencies is easily calculated. Indeed, the circular current, generated by the input devices, is amplified by B and flows in the output load. The output resistance R n4 at node 4 is quite high. Actually it is the only high resistance in the circuit. All other nodes are at the 1/g m level. As a result, this is a single-stage amplifier: there is only one high-resistance node, one single node where the gain is large, where the swing is large, and ultimately where the dominant pole is formed. The voltage gain at low frequencies is now easily obtained. The bandwidth is created at the same output node. The GBW is the product. It is the same as for a single-transistor amplifier but multiplied by current factor B. Increasing B increases the GBW. How far can we go with B?"
    }
